1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,127
One shape. Start with a single tile, and keep going.

2
00:00:04,527 --> 00:00:09,046
Every tile is the same hat. The gold ones are just flipped over.

3
00:00:09,396 --> 00:00:17,103
It covers the whole floor, with no gaps and no overlaps. But the pattern never repeats, however far you go.

4
00:00:17,453 --> 00:00:25,968
Count them. For every flipped hat, there are about six point eight five normal ones: the golden ratio, to the fourth power.

5
00:00:26,318 --> 00:00:31,465
It was found in 2023 by David Smith, an amateur from Yorkshire.

6
00:00:31,965 --> 00:00:39,436
But look closer. The hats secretly team up into clusters, and every gold hat sits in a cluster of four.

7
00:00:39,836 --> 00:00:49,240
The clusters team up again. Four hats, then twenty five, then one hundred and sixty nine, then one thousand one hundred and fifty six.

8
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:58,730
Two squared, five squared, thirteen squared, thirty four squared. Squares of Fibonacci numbers, hiding in the floor.

9
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:05,165
Bigger and bigger copies, forever. That ladder is why it can never repeat.

10
00:01:05,515 --> 00:01:08,754
So which other shapes hide a ladder like this?

